--\nThe major updates from BatPaC v4.0 to BatPaC v5.0 are as follows: (https://publications.anl.gov/anlpubs/2022/07/176234.pdf)\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 146; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view2\",\"pattern\":\"3.1\"}); Total lines: 4457\nL65@P3: 3.1 Background and General Instructions................................................................ 12\nL66@P3: 3.2 Dashboard Worksheet........................................................................................ 13\nL67@P3: 3.2.1 Dashboard Ribbon ............................................................................................. 13\nL68@P3: 3.2.2 Dashboard Inputs............................................................................................... 15\nL93@P4: 5.2.5 Cell Dimensions................................................................................................. 58\nL94@P4: 5.3 Module Design................................................................................................... 62\nL95@P4: 5.3.1 Module Dimensions........................................................................................... 62\nL96@P4: 5.3.2 Module Component Design ............................................................................... 64\nL117@P5: 8.1 Overview of Thermal Management System ...................................................... 88\nL118@P5: 8.2 Coolant Flow System......................................................................................... 89\nL119@P5: 8.3 Additions for Coolant Management................................................................... 90\nL120@P5: 8.3.1 Cooling Requirements ....................................................................................... 91\nL133@P6: 9.2.2 Purchased Items Costs ..................................................................................... 102\nL134@P6: 9.2.3 Pack Integration Cost....................................................................................... 103\nL135@P6: 9.3 Baseline Manufacturing Plant.......................................................................... 104\nL136@P6: 9.3.1 Electrode Processing........................................................................................ 108\nL707@P30: 3.1 Background and General Instructions\nL708@P30: The model is based on the use of Microsoft® Office Excel spreadsheets. The following subsections\nL709@P30: provide an explanation on how to operate the spreadsheet-based model. The user is advised to\nL710@P30: save the original document separately as a back-up copy. Corruption of the calculations is\nL711@P30: possible and will likely occur during use by someone unfamiliar with the model.\nL712@P30: This Microsoft® Office Excel workbook requires the use of iteration. The iterative function may\nL713@P30: be enabled by going to File > Options > Formulas. Check the box next to “Iteration” and change\nL714@P30: the maximum number of iterations to 1000 (Figure 3.1). (The latter change is less important for\nL715@P30: this version than in the past because the calculations almost always come to the final answer in\nL716@P30: well under the default Excel value of 100 iterations.) If the iteration setting is not turned on, the\nL717@P30: software will present an error complaining about circular references. If the model is opened while\nL718@P30: a different Excel spreadsheet is in use, the software may also warn of an error.\nL719@P30-31: Figure 3.1. Automatic iteration must be enabled for the spreadsheet model to function13\nL720@P31: In addition, several features and tools require macros to be enabled in the workbook. While they\nL721@P31: are not required to run the model, they improve the functionality. To enable macros in Excel, select\nL722@P31: “Developer” in the ribbon (Figure 3.2). Then choose “Macro Security” and make sure “Enable all\nL723@P31: macros” is selected in the pop-up window that appears. Click “OK” once this is confirmed.\nL724@P31: Figure 3.2. Image showing how to enable macros in Microsoft Excel.\nL725@P31: 3.2 Dashboard Worksheet\nL726@P31: Most of the functionality of BatPaC can be controlled through the ‘Dashboard’ worksheet. This\nL727@P31: worksheet includes commonly altered user inputs, a summary of key outputs, and possible error\nL728@P31: warnings associated with the battery design. This section provides a description of ‘Dashboard’\nL785@P33: more favorable designs when the power-to-energy ratio of the pack is greater than 15. Table 3.1\nL786@P33: provides an overview of the property differences between the two couples. The power couples are\nL787@P33: better suited at high-power because they have lower area-specific impedances (ASI), which\nL788@P33: correspond to lower voltage losses during high-current (i.e., high-power) discharges. The energy\nL789@P33: couples are better suited for high-energy applications because they are more energy-dense,\nL790@P33: resulting in more compact cell designs. See the overview of the ‘Chem’ worksheet in Section 3.4\nL791@P33-34: for more information.16\nL792@P34: Figure 3.5. Chemistry inputs on 'Dashboard'\nL793@P34: Table 3.1. Overview of electrode design changes for default power and energy couples\nL794@P34: Power Energy\nL795@P34: Positive active material particle radius, µm\nL796@P34:  Manganese spinel and layered oxides†\nL797@P34:  Lithium-iron-phosphate (LFP)\nL798@P34: 1.00\nL799@P34: 0.25\nL800@P34: 2.00\nL801@P34: 0.50\nL802@P34: Negative active material particle radius, µm\nL803@P34:  Graphite\nL804@P34:  Lithium-titanate (LTO)\nL805@P34: 4.00\nL806@P34: 0.25\nL807@P34: 6.00\nL808@P34: 0.50\nL809@P34: Porosity (both electrodes) 0.25 0.35\nL810@P34: Current collector thickness, µm\nL811@P34:  Positive (aluminum)\nL812@P34:  Negative (copper)††\nL813@P34: 20\nL814@P34: 15\nL815@P34: 15\nL816@P34: 10\nL817@P34: Positive wt.% (active:carbon:binder) 94:4:2 96:2:2\nL818@P34: Negative wt.% (active:carbon:binder)\nL819@P34:  Graphite\nL820@P34:  Lithium-titanate (LTO)\nL821@P34: 96:2:2\nL822@P34: 94:4:2\nL823@P34: 98:0:2\nL824@P34: 96:2:2\nL825@P34: †\nL826@P34: nickel-cobalt-aluminum oxide (NCA), nickel-manganese-cobalt oxide (NMC), and lithium\nL827@P34: manganese oxide (LMO)\nL828@P34: †† aluminum used for LTO\nL834@P35: maximum allowable positive electrode thickness. This value impacts the battery design and is\nL835@P35: discussed in detail in Section 5. All other chemistry-related values (including several in Table 3.1)\nL836@P35: can be overridden in the ‘Chem’ worksheet.\nL837@P35: 3.2.2.2 Battery Design Inputs\nL838@P35: The battery design section (Figure 3.6) on ‘Dashboard’ provides all the input parameters for\nL839@P35: designing the battery. The first section is general information, where the user can change the\nL926@P39: in house and the cost includes profits and warranty.\nL927@P39: 3.2.3 Dashboard Results\nL928@P39: 3.2.3.1 Calculation and Validation Results\nL929@P39: The first section of the results in ‘Dashboard’ provides validation of the calculation (Figure 3.9a).\nL930@P39: It starts with configuration errors and warnings, which appear if BatPaC detects issues in the\nL931@P39: inputs. If an issue is detected, a number will appear in either the errors or warnings row. The\nL1259@P51: It is possible, although highly unlikely, that the user may select a set of input variables that does\nL1260@P51: not immediately lead to a solution. In that case, the user should consider adjusting the convergence\nL1261@P51: constants and initial estimates for that study. Recall that most issues with BatPaC can be solved\nL1262@P51: by resetting the calculation (refer to Section 3.2.1). When the calculation is reset, BatPaC sets the\nL1263@P51: three parameters to their initial estimates. Then, the iterative solver in excel (see Section 3.1)\nL1297@P53: reversible gravimetric capacities of the active materials (mAh/g) and open circuit voltage (OCV)\nL12