s for this warning. For instance, three rows of modules\nL2884@P108-109: drop, and pumping power.91\nL2885@P109: 8.3.1 Cooling Requirements\nL2886@P109: The total cooling requirement for the pack is the sum of two values: the heat generation during\nL2887@P109: sustained operation (qs) and the rate of heat transfer from hot ambient conditions (qamb).\nL2888@P109: 8.3.1.1 Heat Generation During Operation\nL2889@P109: In BatPaC, the heat generation during sustained operation (qs) is calculated as follows:\nL2890@P109: 𝑞𝑠 = 𝐼𝑠\nL2891@P109: 2𝑅𝑠 (8.1)\nL2892@P109: where IS and RS are the current (A) and resistance (Ω), respectively, during a sustained discharge.\nL2962@P110: for the cooling targets since the thermal mass of the battery evens out temperature fluctuations\nL2963@P110-111: from power bursts.93\nL2964@P111: 8.3.1.2 Heat Transfer from Ambient\nL2965@P111: The rate of heat transfer from hot ambient conditions is calculated using the following equation:\nL2966@P111: 𝑞𝑎𝑚𝑏 =\nL2967@P111: 𝑘𝑖𝑛𝑠𝑢𝑙𝑎𝑡𝑖𝑜𝑛𝐴𝑖𝑛𝑠𝑢𝑙𝑎𝑡𝑖𝑜𝑛\nL2968@P111: 𝛿𝑖𝑛𝑠𝑢𝑙𝑎𝑡𝑖𝑜𝑛\nL2969@P111: (𝑇𝐻,𝑎𝑚𝑏 − 𝑇) (8.10)\nL2970@P111: where k, A, and δ are the thermal conductivity (default = 0.00027 W/cm/K), area, and thickness\nL3045@P112: ‘Dashboard’. Table 8.3 provides the temperature ranges for these numbers. The grades for each\nL3046@P112: battery design are included in the ‘Thermal’ worksheet and the Calculation and Validation section\nL3047@P112-113: within ‘Dashboard’ (see Section 3.2.3.1).95\nL3048@P113: Table 8.3. Temperature ranges for determining adequacy of cooling\nL3049@P113: Adequacy of Cooling Tmax - T During Sustained Discharge (°C)\nL3050@P113: Excellent <5\nL3051@P113: Good 5 to 10\nL3052@P113: Fair 10 to 15\nL3053@P113: Poor >15\nL3653@P125: Formation cycling, testing, and sealing 560,000 830 110,000\nL3654@P125: Module assembly 170,000 94 27,000\nL3655@P125: Pack assembly and testing 150,000 94 27,000\nL3656@P125: Warehouse 31,000 200 10,000\nL3657@P125: Building and supporting systems\nL3658@P125:  Building\nL3659@P125:  Solvent recovery\nL3660@P125:  Rejected cell and scrap recycle\nL3661@P125:  Control laboratory\nL3662@P125: -\nL3663@P125: 17,000\nL3664@P125: 38,000\nL3665@P125: 46,000\nL3666@P125: 1,700\nL3667@P125: 36\nL3668@P125: 9.3\nL3669@P125: 16\nL3670@P125: -\nL3671@P125: 1,100\nL3672@P125: 3,300\nL3673@P125: 1,300\nL3674@P125: Total 3,876,000 4,222 305,700\nL3675@P125: †\nL3676@P125: both electrodes\nL3677@P125-126: *cost includes installation108\nL3678@P126: 9.3.1 Electrode Processing\nL3679@P126: 9.3.1.1 Materials Preparation and Mixing\nL3685@P126: respectively. A 30% head space is assumed for all machines. The labor is calculated assuming 1.5\nL3686@P126: operators per machine per shift. The capital equipment costs and plant area for this step also factor\nL3687@P126: in hoppers for materials handling, side mixers for the solvent-binder mixtures, storage vessels for\nL3688@P126: transportation to the coater, and filtration systems for de-ionized water.\nL3689@P126: 9.3.1.2 Electrode Coating\nL3690@P126: The positive and negative electrodes are coated to the current collector foils using double-sided,\nL3700@P126: viscosity control, a surface defect detector, an insulation coating unit (ceramic and/or polyamide\u0002imide), and an auto splitting unit. The auto splitting unit is used to split the two stripes into two\nL3701@P126: separate rolls to be fed into the calendering machine (next section). Automated guided vehicles for\nL3702@P126: handling the rolls are also included in the cost. The baseline rates for both electrodes can be\nL3703@P126: accommodated with five coaters each, even though the negative area is slightly larger than the\nL3704@P126: positive. The costs associated with the positive electrode are higher than the negative in Table 9.6\nL3705@P126: due to the use of NMP in the positive, which must be condensed and recycled (see Section 9.3.7.2).\nL3706@P126: It is assumed that 0.5% of the NMP is lost during coating. The labor rates assume 1.5 operators\nL3707@P126: per coater per shift.\nL3708@P126: 9.3.1.3 Calendering\nL3709@P126: After coating, the electrodes move to the calendering machines, where they are compressed by\nL3710@P126: rolling to meet the specified void volume fraction. The baseline rates for the positive and negative\nL3711@P126: electrodes can be accommodated with eight and nine calenders, respectively. Each calender\nL3712@P126: operates at 100 m/min. The calenders require a larger m/min throughput than the coaters because\nL3713@P126: of the increase in total roll length when the rolls were split after coating. All calenders include\nL3714@P126: laser automatic thickness gauges. The per-piece size and cost of the positive calenders are larger\nL3715@P126-127: than the negatives in Table 9.6 because the positive calenders require pre-heating and induction 109\nL3716@P127: heating annealing to perform the calendering at elevated temperatures. The negative calenders\nL3717@P127: operate at room temperature. The labor rates in Table 9.6 correspond to one operator per calender\nL3718@P127: per shift.\nL3719@P127: 9.3.1.4 Notching\nL3720@P127: Notching machines slit the coated and calendered electrode foils into two electrode strips using a\nL3721@P127: die cutter. Simultaneously, current collector tabs are cut using precision lasers on the uncoated\nL3722@P127: portion of the foil. Figure 9.2 highlights the regions which are cut during this process. The\nL3723@P127: estimated scrap loss of foil for this process is ~8%. The baseline rates can be accommodated with\nL3724@P127: 34 machines each for the positive and negative materials (68 total) operating at 24 m/min. The\nL3725@P127: labor rate is calculated assuming one operator per notching machine per shift.\nL3726@P127: Figure 9.2. Schematic of electrodes coated on current collector foil showing regions that are cut\nL3727@P127: after coating and during notching.\nL3728@P127: 9.3.1.5 Vacuum Drying of Electrodes\nL3729@P127: Moisture from the electrodes must be removed prior to cell assembly to prevent harmful reactions\nL3730@P127: between the electrolyte and water. The reels of notched electrodes are dried at elevated\nL3731@P127: temperatures in vacuum drying ovens. Five and four vacuum dryers are required for the positive\nL3909@P132: Produced\nL3910@P132: C/Co = (R/Ro)\nL3911@P132: p-1\nL3912@P132: Scale Factor, p R/Ro = 2 R/Ro = 3 R/Ro = 2 R/Ro = 3\nL3913@P132: 0.25 1.19 1.32 0.596 0.439\nL3914@P132: 0.3 1.23 1.39 0.616 0.463\nL3915@P132: 0.4 1.32 1.55 0.660 0.517\nL3916@P132: 0.5 1.41 1.73 0.707 0.577\nL3917@P132: 0.6 1.52 1.93 0.758 0.644\nL3918@P132: 0.7 1.62 2.16 0.812 0.719\nL3919@P132: 0.8 1.74 2.41 0.871 0.803\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe major updates from BatPaC v4.0 to BatPaC v5.0 are as follows: (https://publications.anl.gov/anlpubs/2022/07/176234.pdf)\nciteturn3view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 146; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view2\",\"pattern\":\"Table 5.1\"}); Total lines: 4457\nL1537@P65: how the connections between modules and packs are sized. Section 5.7 provides information on\nL1538@P65: how the vehicle type impacts the default input parameters used in the calculation.\nL1539@P65: 5.1 Overview of Battery Design\nL1540@P65: Figure 5.1 shows a schematic of the user-defined inputs and battery design outputs from the\nL1541@P65: BatPaC model. BatPaC uses an iterative process to generate a cell design that satisfies all user\nL1542@P65: inputs. The resulting cell design is used to calculate the dimensions and performance of the\nL1543@P65: module(s) and pack(s). The goal of the model is to quantify how the various components of a\nL1544@P65: specific battery design sum to make the mass and volume of the pack. In this way, a true energy\nL1545@P65: and power density can be calculated as well as the exact materials requirement to meet the design.\nL1546@P65: Figure 5.1. Schematic summary of the battery design model.\nL1547@P65: In the figure, the user-defined inputs are broken into three main categories: performance, cell\nL1635@P67: where ASIP is the area-specific impedance (Ω cm2) of the cell at rated power. The ASIs (ASIP and\nL1636@P67: ASIE) depend on the electrode thickness (δpos), electrode area (Apos), and applied current density (I)\nL1637@P67: of the cell. The calculation is described in detail in Section 5.2.2. For now, it is represented using\nL1638@P67: the following expression:\nL1639@P67-68: 𝐴𝑆𝐼 = 𝑓(𝛿𝑝𝑜𝑠, 𝐴𝑝𝑜𝑠,𝐼) (5.5)50\nL1640@P68: Equations 5.1 to 5.5 represent a set of six algebraic equations (Eq. 5.5 is used twice) with six\nL1641@P68: unknowns: Ccell, Apos, δpos, Ip, ASIP, and ASIE. To properly bound these equations, recall that BatPaC\nL1642@P68: requires the user to input the total pack energy (Epack, Wh) or the total pack capacity (Cpack, Ah) to\nL1643@P68: define the size of the pack. If Ebatt is specified, the formulation above is unchanged and equations\nL1644@P68: 5.1 to 5.5 can be solved to calculate the unknowns, with Eq. 5.5 used twice for ASIP and ASIE. If\nL1645@P68: Cpack is specified, equation 5.1 is replaced by equation 5.6.\nL1646@P68: 𝐶𝑝𝑎𝑐𝑘 = 𝐶𝑐𝑒𝑙𝑙𝑁𝑚𝑜𝑑,||𝑁𝑐𝑒𝑙𝑙𝑠,|| (5.6)\nL1647@P68: where Nmod,|| and Ncells,|| are the number of modules in parallel within the pack and the number of\nL1648@P68: cells in parallel within a module, respectively. Both are user-defined inputs. Note that, in this case,\nL1649@P68: Eq. 5.1 is still used by BatPaC to output the calculated energy, but it is no longer required to solve\nL1650@P68: the coupled equations.\nL1651@P68: 5.2.2 ASI Model\nL1652@P68: As noted in the previous section, area-specific impedance (ASI) is an important characteristic of a\nL1974@P75: note that equations 5.1 to 5.5 all depend on the properties of the positive electrode. The properties\nL1975@P75: of the negative electrode are calculated from these output values. The negative electrode area is\nL1976@P75: calculated by assuming it is 1 mm larger than the positive electrode in both length and width to\nL1977@P75: alleviate concerns of lithium plating during charge pulses. In addition, the separator area is slightly\nL1978@P75: larger than the negative electrode to prevent the electrical shorting of the two electrodes.\nL1995@P76: When the maximum electrode thickness, δmax, has been reached on either the positive or negative\nL1996@P76: electrode, the electrode area equation is modified as shown in Equation 5.30.\nL1997@P76: 𝐴𝑝𝑜𝑠,𝛿𝑚𝑎𝑥 =\nL1998@P76: 𝛿𝑡𝑔𝑡\nL1999@P76: 𝛿𝑚𝑎𝑥\nL2000@P76: 𝐴𝑝𝑜𝑠 (5.30)\nL2001@P76: where the electrode thickness, δtgt, is the largest electrode thickness, negative or positive,\nL2002@P76: determined by solving the governing equations (5.1 to 5.5). The area of the electrode is now\nL2003@P76: determined by the cell capacity requirement to meet the battery energy demands and not the rated\nL2004@P76: power. To elaborate, if δtgt > δmax, the model completes one more design iteration. For the final\nL2005@P76: iteration, Eq. 5.30 replaces equations 5.3 and 5.4 with δtgt and Apos as constants. The model then\nL2006@P76: reduces to a set of four governing equations (5.1, 5.2, 5.5, and 5.30) with four unknowns (Ccell,\nL2007@P76: 𝐴𝑝𝑜𝑠,𝛿𝑚𝑎𝑥, ASIE, and IE).\nL2008@P76: Consequently, to maintain the same input power rating, the designed battery will operate at a\nL2009@P76: smaller voltage drop than the maximum allowed by the user. The new [V/U]P may then be\nL2345@P90: The values of the input parameters used to calculate the power and energy of the battery pack\nL2346@P90: depend on the vehicle type being simulated. The values change because the different vehicle types\nL2347@P90: are operated at different states of charge and have different power requirements. BatPaC provides\nL2348@P90: default values for these input parameters based on the operating conditions of the vehicle.\nL2349@P90: Table 5.1 summarizes the operating conditions assumed by the model for the four vehicle types:\nL2350@P90: mild hybrid-electric vehicle (mild-HEV), high-power hybrid-electric vehicle (HEV-HP), plug-in\nL2351@P90: hybrid-electric vehicle (PHEV), and electric vehicle (EV). The table also lists which input\nL2352@P90: parameters are impacted by the assumed operating conditions. In addition, a default cell thickness\nL2353@P90: for each vehicle type is included in\nL2354@P90: Table 5.1, which is based on industry standards observed by the BatPaC group. In general, cells\nL2355@P90: become thinner for vehicles with higher power to energy ratios where thermal management is more\nL2356@P90: of a concern. The remainder of this section provides a brief description of the rationale behind the\nL2357@P90: values.\nL2358@P90: The first two driving conditions are the upper and lower SOC limits during operation. These values\nL2359@P90: are also highlighted in Figure 5.12, which provides a schematic of the SOC window. The upper\nL2360@P90: and lower SOC values are determined to provide the desired pack performance and degradation\nL2361@P90: rate for each vehicle type. These values do not directly impact the calculation; however, they are\nL2362@P90: used by BatPaC to calculate the useable pack energy (i.e., energy between SOC limits, see Figure\nL2363@P90: 5.12). This is an important metric for pack design. For packs where range is important (PHEVs\nL2364@P90: and EVs), the useable energy can be combined with the Wh/mile of the vehicle to determine the\nL2365@P90-91: total pack size required to reach a certain driving range. The total pack size is a key input parameter 73\nL2366@P91: in ‘Dashboard.’ The lower SOC limit is also used by BatPaC to guide user design when the SOC\nL2367@P91: and duration at full power are overridden. BatPaC provides a check to make sure the pack does\nL2368@P91: not go below the SOC limit during the pulse and provides an error if this occurs. BatPaC also\nL2369@P91: provides a less severe warning if the SOC goes below a reserve safety limit (see Figure 5.12). The\nL2370@P91: reserve safety limit is included in most vehicles to guarantee consistent performance of the vehicle.\nL2371@P91: Note that the default lower SOC limits correspond to graphite negative electrodes, where the risk\nL2372@P91: of lithium metal plating restricts the useable battery energy. For other materials, like lithium\nL2373@P91: titanate spinel (Li4Ti5O12, LTO), it may be prudent to lower this number for PHEVs and EVs.\nL2374@P91: Table 5.1. Default operating conditions and input parameters for pre-defined vehicle types\nL2375@P91: Vehicle Type Mild-HEV HEV-HP PHEV EV Impacted Input\nL2376@P91: Upper SOC limit 95 90 90 95 -\nL2377@P91: Lower SOC limit 70 30 20 10 -\nL2378@P91: SOC at average operation, % 80 80 50 50 UE, ASIE\nL2379@P91: SOC for at rated power, % 80 50 80 20 UP, ASIP\nL2380@P91: Duration at rated power, sec 2 10 10 10 ASIP\nL2381@P91: Cell Thickness, mm 6 12 16 20 δcell\nL2382@P91: Figure 5.12 Schematic of the usable energy for a battery and the locations of the state of charge\nL2383@P91: (SOC) for average operation and the SOC at full power. The locations of the SOCs do not\nL2384@P91: correspond to a specific vehicle type.\nL2385@P91: The second driving condition that impacts the model inputs is the state of charge at average\nL2386@P91: operation, which is also the SOC for rated energy. This condition impacts the default, user-defined\nL2387@P91-92: inputs for the open circuit voltage (UE) and area-specific resistance (ASIE) used to determine the 74\nL2388@P92: battery energy in Eq. 5.1. For PHEVs and EVs, these inputs are taken at 50% SOC. For mild\u0002HEVs and HEV-HPs, the battery operates at a higher state of charge, where the cell voltage and\nL2389@P92: ASI are favorable for operation at high power. Therefore, the values of UE and ASIE are taken at\n"
